    "result_text": "Done. The full build is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 2/2 tests passing).\n\n## What I did\n\nThe `locality` field is derived from an owner's city and returned on `GET /api/owners/{id}`. The owner response is produced by MapStruct (`OwnerMapper.toOwnerDto`) from the generated `OwnerDto` (from `openapi.yml`), so I:\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `locality` property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries the field.\n2. **`Locality.java`** (new, in `rest/function/owner`) \u2014 the fixed table `Sydney\u2192NSW, Melbourne\u2192VIC, Brisbane\u2192QLD`, returning `\"UNKNOWN\"` for any other (or null) city.\n3. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 a `@Mapping(target = \"locality\", expression = ...)` calling `Locality.of(owner.getCity())`.\n\n## One trap worth noting\n\nMy first attempt put the `deriveLocality(String)` helper *inside* the `OwnerMapper` interface. MapStruct treated it as a general `String\u2192String` mapping method and silently wired it into **every** String property (telephone, address, email, \u2026), which turned a normalized telephone into `\"UNKNOWN\"` and blew up owner creation with a `ConstraintViolationException` (E.164 validation) \u2014 failing at the create step before the locality assertion was ever reached. Moving the helper into a standalone `Locality` class, referenced only via the mapping expression, keeps MapStruct from auto-adopting it.",
